  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule are both created, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**1. Fields in the data view.** Each document in `kibana_sample_data_logs` is one web request. The time field is `timestamp`. The useful fields are:\n- **What was requested:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`\n- **The result:** `response`, the HTTP status code (stored as a string, with a `.keyword` version), and `bytes`, the response size\n- **Who sent it:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`\n- **Where from and to:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `memory`, `phpmemory`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-58b857ca Web logs overview\"** (id `b880b5a5-fed1-427f-9c0e-85f2e77fbf92`, default time range is the last 7 days). It has seven panels:\n- Four headline numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average bytes per response, and the percentage of requests that returned a 5xx code.\n- Requests over time, stacked by response code.\n- The 10 most requested URLs.\n- Traffic from the 10 top source countries.\n\nThe last four panels use ES|QL queries. I couldn't test those queries against Elasticsearch, because the Kibana console proxy is turned off here. Kibana accepted the dashboard, but nothing has been checked in a browser. My first version of the error-rate query was wrong, and I corrected it before finishing. If any panel shows an error when you open it, that will be the place to look.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-58b857ca 5xx spike\"** (id `0db4bda7-eb0f-405b-8b09-21db8145b179`):\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" in the last 5 minutes, based on `timestamp`, and fires when the count is above 10.\n- I turned off \"exclude hits from previous run\" so that each check counts everything in the full 5-minute window.\n- It's enabled, and no notification actions are attached, so it will create alerts but won't send them anywhere.\n\nThe request bodies I sent are saved in the scratchpad as `dash.json` and `rule.json`.",
